        "text": "Electrophilic Aromatic Substitution Reactions"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00001",
          "segmentId": "m00209:para:para-00001"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00001",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00209:alt:fig-00001-alt",
              "text": "Phenol reacts with an electrophile to form ortho-substituted phenol and para-substituted phenol."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_10_001.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00209:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Oxidation of Phenols: Quinones"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00209:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00209:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "Phenol reacts with sodium dichromate to form benzoquinone with 79 percent yield. The electrostatic potential map of benzoquinone shows carbon, hydrogen and oxygen as black, gray and red spheres, respectively."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_10_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00209:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00209:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "A reversible reaction in which benzoquinone reacts with tin(2) chloride, water, and sodium dichromate to form hydroquinone."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_10_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00209:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00209:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "The structure of ubiquinones. One substituent has a repeating unit that occurs 1 to 10 times (n equals 1 to 10)."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_10_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00005",
          "segmentId": "m00209:para:para-00005"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00005",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00209:alt:fig-00005-alt",
              "text": "Two steps in which ubiquinone functions as an intermediate to convert nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide hydrogen to nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide plus ion by oxidation."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_10_005.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
